WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:11.560
Good morning, Hank!

00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:12.440
Hi, Hank!

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:13.520
Good morning, Hank!

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:16.020
Hi, Hank! Hello!

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:16.940
Good morning, Hank.

00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:18.640
Good morning, Hank!

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:20.440
I can't just say good morning, Hank.

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:21.400
JOHN: Julie.

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:22.040
Yes?

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:23.840
JOHN: Will you say "Good morning, Hank?"

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:27.260
I'm gonna make Good Morning Hank above 700 librarians?

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:29.260
Will you say... will you just?

00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:30.340
Good morning, Hank.

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:34.340
Not above 700 librarians, Hank but, alongside with them.

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:38.260
CROWD:
Good morning, Hank. It's Tuesday.

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:40.340
That was a pretty great "Good morning, Hank!"

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:44.400
Hank, a lot of great things happened at the American Library Association conference this weekend,

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:49.200
And if you're wondering why you're looking at this very strange shot instead of any of those great things

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:54.200
like me hanging out with Judy Blume, or me going to a lot of fun parties,

00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:58.540
It's because I accidentally taped over all that stuff with 30 minutes of this stuff.

00:00:58.559 --> 00:00:59.770
AAAAAAAAGGGHH

00:00:59.770 --> 00:01:00.940
Stupid!

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:03.660
Fortunately you will get to listen to me harangue a reader.

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:04.420
JOHN: Are you a dumper?

00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:05.140
WOMAN: No.

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:07.500
JOHN: You're only friends 'cuz he wants you back.

00:01:07.510 --> 00:01:09.460
WOMAN: No it was mutual, actually.

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:10.340
JOHN: It's never mutual!
WOMAN: I was just the first-

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:12.779
In fact pretty much the only things I have footage of are

00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:15.900
Nerdfighter Cynthia Lord winning the Newberry Honor

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:17.500
for her book "Rules,"

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:19.780
And the Printz Award reception on Monday night.

00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:25.840
SPEAKER: When I returned from midwinter in Seattle, I was alerted to check out the Brotherhood 2.0 website

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:30.720
[applause and laughter]

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:38.560
Where John got a video of THE phone call. It was fun to see his happy dance over the good news,

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:42.240
and tune in again the next day to see John's brother, Hank, toast him with

00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:50.400
"Here's to silver medals — to... not quite doing as good as you did last time!" [audience laughter]

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:58.600
Hank, ALA Convention is always fun, but this one was particularly fun because it was so Hank-tastic.

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:00.400
Everywhere I went, people were like

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:01.920
"Hey, you're Hank Green's brother!"

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:02.820
And I was like

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:04.600
"Yeah, I also write books!" And they were like

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:06.780
"...right, but you're Hank Green's brother!!"

00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:09.940
Four different people asked me if I knew your phone number, and I was like

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:14.000
"He's my brother. Yes, I know his phone number."

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:16.940
And they said, "Can I have it?" And I said, "No!"

00:02:16.940 --> 00:02:21.420
People kept telling me how cute you are, and how funny you are, and how much they like you.

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:23.720
So, Hank, if you like young adult literature so much,

00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:27.440
I would encourage you to write some of it, because you're bound to be an instant hit.

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:28.660
All the Printz features were great.

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:33.200
Markus Zusak, Tobin Anderson, Gene Yang, and Sonya Hartnett all were wonderful.

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:36.780
I don't know how I did, but here's the funniest snippet. I'll let you judge.

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:41.040
People still think of librarians as these shy, retiring flowers.

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:43.660
Whenever I hear someone stereotype a librarian like that,

00:02:43.660 --> 00:02:48.160
I always counter their inherited story with a true one of my own. This is entirely true.

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:52.600
I say that last year, at a librarian conference in New Orleans,

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:57.640
I learned how hard librarians party during an evening of casual gambling. [audience chuckles]

00:02:57.640 --> 00:02:59.700
When I happened across a librarian

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:01.360
passed out drunk,

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:04.260
sprawled across the loud carpet of Harrah's Casino,

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:06.400
a spilled drink in one hand,

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:13.160
and a galley of a promising first novel in the other. [laughter and applause]

00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:14.860
Hank, I'll see you tomorrow.

